<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-descriptive-statistics_1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Descriptive Statistics</title>
<!--  ======================================================================  -->
<!--  SECTION 1: THE STORY  -->
<!--  ======================================================================  -->
<section xml:id="sec-story">
<title>1. The Story: Motivating Example and Exploratory Data Analysis</title>
<introduction>
<p>
In traditional statistics education, students spend weeks memorizing probability axioms,
<m>z</m>
-scores, and standard normal tables before encountering a real dataset. As statistician George Cobb famously argued, this structure is a relic of nineteenth-century computational limitations—a "Ptolemaic curriculum" with epicycles of mathematical approximations required only because researchers lacked computers to shuffle and simulate data directly.
</p>
<p>
Here, we invert the paradigm:
<m>\textbf{the data come first}</m>
. Before we formulate mathematical models or write a single line of software code, we immerse ourselves in the empirical phenomena, observe the natural variation through visualization, and articulate questions grounded in biology.
</p>
</introduction>
<subsection xml:id="subsec-ecological-mystery">
<title>The Ecological Mystery: Honest Signals in Greater Flamingos</title>
<p>
Greater Flamingos (
<m>\textit{Phoenicopterus roseus}</m>
) inhabiting the Rift Valley lakes of East Africa—such as Lake Nakuru, Lake Bogoria, and Lake Natron—exhibit striking variation in their plumage coloration, ranging from pale dusty pink to brilliant crimson. Why does this variation exist, and what are its evolutionary consequences?
</p>
<p>
Ornithologists know that flamingos cannot synthesize pink carotenoid pigments de novo; they must ingest them by filter-feeding on microscopic blue-green algae, primarily
<m>\textit{Arthrospira platensis}</m>
(
<m>\text{Spirulina}</m>
), and tiny aquatic crustaceans thriving in saline, alkaline waters. Furthermore, evolutionary biologists hypothesize that plumage coloration functions as an
<m>\textbf{honest signal}</m>
of physical quality during group courtship rituals (known as "marching displays").
</p>
<p>
This leads us to two core empirical questions that drive our investigation:
<ol>
<li>
<p>
<m>\textbf{The Foraging Mechanistic Question}</m>
: Does the density of dietary
<m>\text{Spirulina}</m>
in a flamingo's habitat, together with the anatomical filtration capacity of its beak (measured by lamellae density), explain observed variation in carotenoid plumage coloration?
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\textbf{The Behavioral & Evolutionary Question}</m>
: Does a bird's carotenoid plumage coloration directly predict its courtship display intensity and mating success across different alkaline lakes?
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="subsec-dataset-structure">
<title>The Flamingo Dataset Structure</title>
<p>
To investigate these questions, field ecologists sampled
<m>n = 120</m>
adult Greater Flamingos across three alkaline lakes in Kenya during the breeding season:
<m>\textbf{Lake Nakuru}</m>
(moderate salinity),
<m>\textbf{Lake Bogoria}</m>
(high salinity and hyper-productive algal blooms), and
<m>\textbf{Lake Natron}</m>
(extreme alkaline flats).
</p>
<p> For each sampled individual, researchers recorded seven key attributes: </p>
<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>
<m>\texttt{id}</m>
: Unique alphanumeric band identifier (
<m>\text{e.g., FL-001}</m>
).
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\texttt{lake}</m>
: Categorical factor indicating habitat (
<m>\text{Nakuru}</m>
,
<m>\text{Bogoria}</m>
, or
<m>\text{Natron}</m>
).
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\texttt{salinity\_ppt}</m>
: Water salinity at the primary foraging site, measured in parts per thousand (
<m>\text{ppt}</m>
).
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\texttt{spirulina\_density}</m>
: Local concentration of
<m>\textit{Arthrospira platensis}</m>
in grams per liter (
<m>\text{g/L}</m>
).
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\texttt{beak\_lamellae\_cnt}</m>
: Count of filtration lamellae per square centimeter along the upper mandible.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\texttt{plumage\_index}</m>
: Colorimetric carotenoid plumage intensity index (
<m>0.0 - 100.0</m>
scale, calibrated via spectrophotometry).
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\texttt{display\_score}</m>
: Behavioral courtship display score (
<m>0 - 100</m>
scale), assessed by ethology observers during group marching displays.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
