</table>
<p>
<m>\textbf{2. Bi-variate Scatter: Spirulina Density vs. Plumage Coloration}</m>
: Plotting
<m>\texttt{spirulina\_density}</m>
(
<m>X</m>
-axis) against
<m>\texttt{plumage\_index}</m>
(
<m>Y</m>
-axis) reveals a powerful positive linear association across the entire sample (
<m>r = 0.94</m>
). Crucially, the observations form natural ecological clusters by lake: Lake Nakuru birds occupy the low-density/pale-plumage quadrant, while Lake Natron birds dominate the high-density/crimson-plumage quadrant.
</p>
<p>
<m>\textbf{3. Behavioral Coupling: Plumage Index vs. Courtship Display Score}</m>
: When we examine whether plumage color functions as an honest signal, a scatterplot of
<m>\texttt{plumage\_index}</m>
against
<m>\texttt{display\_score}</m>
reveals a tight linear coupling (
<m>r = 0.96</m>
). Birds with pinker plumage consistently perform more vigorous and frequent courtship displays.
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig-eda-conceptual">
<caption>Conceptual Schematic of the Three Motivating Exploratory Plots in the Flamingo Investigation</caption>
<image width="90%">
<description> Panel A: Side-by-side boxplots showing Plumage Index increasing from Nakuru (mean 48.4) to Bogoria (mean 76.1) to Natron (mean 89.4). Panel B: Strong positive scatterplot of Spirulina Density vs. Plumage Index showing clear group clustering by Lake. Panel C: Direct linear scatterplot showing Plumage Index predicting Courtship Display Score across all 120 individuals. </description>
</image>
</figure>
<p>
These visualizations establish our empirical reality: there are profound differences in plumage coloration across lakes, and plumage color is tightly linked to dietary
<m>\text{Spirulina}</m>
density and courtship performance. But as scientists, we must ask:
<m>\textbf{Could these patterns have arisen merely by chance variation?}</m>
How do we formally quantify the strength and significance of these relationships without resorting to black-box formulas?
</p>
</subsection>
</section>
<!--  ======================================================================  -->
<!--  SECTION 2: THE METHODS  -->
<!--  ======================================================================  -->
<section xml:id="sec-methods">
<title>2. The Methods: Mathematical and Simulation-Based Statistical Framework</title>
<introduction>
<p>
This section presents the mathematical and conceptual foundations of statistical inference. In keeping with George Cobb's curriculum, we decouple statistical reasoning from software syntax. We first introduce
<m>\textbf{Simulation-Based Inference}</m>
(Cobb's "Three Rs": Randomize, Repeat, Reject), which makes the logic of p-values and confidence intervals physically transparent. We then connect this computational intuition to classical
<m>\textbf{Parametric Linear Modeling}</m>
(Ordinary Least Squares regression and ANOVA decomposition).
</p>
</introduction>
<subsection xml:id="subsec-cobb-three-rs">
<title>Simulation-Based Inference: Cobb's "Three Rs" (Randomize, Repeat, Reject)</title>
<p> Why do we use permutation tests and bootstrapping? Traditional parametric tests rely on central limit theorem approximations that assume specific theoretical distributions. Cobb demonstrated that computers allow us to test hypotheses using the actual observed data through three intuitive steps: </p>
<p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>
<m>\textbf{Randomize}</m>
: Under the null hypothesis (
<m>\nullhyp</m>
) of no relationship between an explanatory variable
<m>X</m>
and a response
<m>Y</m>
, the labels of
<m>X</m>
are arbitrary. We simulate this null world by randomly shuffling (permuting) the values of
<m>X</m>
across the observations.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\textbf{Repeat}</m>
: We repeat this randomization process thousands of times (
<m>M = 10{,}000</m>
), calculating our test statistic of interest—such as a difference in group means
<m>\Delta \bar{y}</m>
or a regression slope
<m>b_1</m>
—for each shuffle to build an
<m>\textbf{empirical null distribution}</m>
.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\textbf{Reject}</m>
: We compare our actual observed test statistic
<m>T_{\text{obs}}</m>
to the empirical null distribution. If
<m>T_{\text{obs}}</m>
falls in the extreme tails (an event rarely seen by chance shuffling), we reject
<m>\nullhyp</m>
.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
<p>
<m>\textbf{Mathematical Formulation of the Permutation P-Value}</m>
: Let
<m>T_{\text{obs}} = t(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y})</m>
be the observed statistic calculated on the sample of size
<m>n</m>
. Let
<m>\pi_m(\mathbf{x})</m>
denote the
<m>m</m>
-th random permutation of the explanatory vector
<m>\mathbf{x}</m>
for
<m>m = 1, 2, \dots, M</m>
. The empirical two-sided permutation p-value
<m>\hat{p}</m>
is given by:
</p>
<me> \hat{p} = \frac{1}{M} \sum_{m=1}^{M} \mathbb{I}\left( \left| t\left(\pi_m(\mathbf{x}), \mathbf{y}\right) \right| \ge \left| T_{\text{obs}} \right| \right) </me>
<p>
where
<m>\mathbb{I}(\cdot)</m>
is the indicator function evaluating to
<m>1</m>
if the permuted statistic is at least as extreme as the observed statistic, and
<m>0</m>
otherwise.
</p>
<p>
<m>\textbf{Non-Parametric Bootstrapping for Confidence Intervals}</m>
: To estimate precision without normality assumptions, we use non-parametric bootstrapping. Given our sample
<m>\mathcal{D} = \{(x_1, y_1), \dots, (x_n, y_n)\}</m>
, we draw
<m>B = 10{,}000</m>
resamples
<m>\mathcal{D}^{*(b)}</m>
of size
<m>n</m>
<m>\textit{with replacement}</m>
from
<m>\mathcal{D}</m>
. For each bootstrap sample, we compute the estimate
<m>\hat{\theta}^{*(b)}</m>
(such as the regression slope
<m>\hat{\beta}_1^*</m>
). A
<m>(1 - \alpha) \times 100\%</m>
percentile bootstrap confidence interval is defined by the empirical quantiles of the bootstrap distribution:
</p>
<me> \text{CI}_{\text{bootstrap}} = \left[ q^*_{\alpha/2}, \quad q^*_{1 - \alpha/2} \right] </me>
</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="subsec-parametric-ols-anova">
<title>Parametric Statistical Modeling: Linear Regression & ANOVA</title>
<p> Once simulation builds intuitive confidence that a relationship is genuine, classical parametric theory provides a powerful framework for continuous prediction and multi-variable attribution. </p>
<p>
<m>\textbf{1. The Multiple Linear Regression Model (OLS)}</m>
: To model how continuous dietary
<m>\text{Spirulina}</m>
density (
<m>X_1</m>
) and beak lamellae count (
<m>X_2</m>
) jointly explain carotenoid plumage index (
<m>Y</m>
), we specify the normal linear model:
</p>
<me> Y_i = \beta_0 + \beta_1 X_{1i} + \beta_2 X_{2i} + \epsilon_i, \quad \epsilon_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2) </me>
<p>
In matrix notation for
<m>n</m>
observations and
<m>p</m>
predictors:
</p>
<me> \mathbf{y} = \mathbf{X}\boldsymbol{\beta} + \boldsymbol{\epsilon}, \quad \boldsymbol{\epsilon} \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, \sigma^2 \mathbf{I}_n) </me>
<p>
The Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) estimator
<m>\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}</m>
minimizes the sum of squared residuals
<m>S(\boldsymbol{\beta}) = (\mathbf{y} - \mathbf{X}\boldsymbol{\beta})^T(\mathbf{y} - \mathbf{X}\boldsymbol{\beta})</m>
, yielding the normal equations solution:
</p>
<me> \hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}} = \left( \mathbf{X}^T \mathbf{X} \right)^{-1} \mathbf{X}^T \mathbf{y} </me>
<p>
The variance-covariance matrix of the estimators is
<m>\Var(\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}) = \sigma^2 (\mathbf{X}^T\mathbf{X})^{-1}</m>
, where
<m>\sigma^2</m>
is unbiasedly estimated by the Mean Squared Residual
<m>s^2 = \frac{\text{SSR}}{n - p - 1}</m>
.
</p>
<p>
<m>\textbf{2. Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) Sum of Squares Decomposition}</m>
: To compare categorical habitat differences (such as mean plumage index across Lake Nakuru, Lake Bogoria, and Lake Natron), we use the One-Way ANOVA decomposition. Total variation in the response is partitioned into variation
<m>\textit{between}</m>
group models and variation
<m>\textit{within}</m>
group residuals:
</p>
<me> \text{SS}_{\text{Total}} = \text{SS}_{\text{Model}} + \text{SS}_{\text{Residual}} </me>
<p>
Mathematically, for
<m>k</m>
groups with sample sizes
<m>n_j</m>
, group means
<m>\bar{y}_j</m>
, and grand mean
<m>\bar{y}</m>
:
</p>
<md>
<mrow> \text{SS}_{\text{Total}} &= \sum_{j=1}^{k} \sum_{i=1}^{n_j} (y_{ij} - \bar{y})^2 </mrow>
<mrow> \text{SS}_{\text{Model}} &= \sum_{j=1}^{k} n_j (\bar{y}_j - \bar{y})^2 </mrow>
<mrow> \text{SS}_{\text{Residual}} &= \sum_{j=1}^{k} \sum_{i=1}^{n_j} (y_{ij} - \bar{y}_j)^2 </mrow>
</md>
<p>
The omnibus null hypothesis
<m>\nullhyp: \mu_{\text{Nakuru}} = \mu_{\text{Bogoria}} = \mu_{\text{Natron}}</m>
is evaluated via the
<m>F</m>
-ratio of Mean Squares:
</p>
<me> F = \frac{\text{MS}_{\text{Model}}}{\text{MS}_{\text{Residual}}} = \frac{\text{SS}_{\text{Model}} / (k - 1)}{\text{SS}_{\text{Residual}} / (n - k)} \sim F_{k-1, n-k} </me>
<p> The proportion of total variance explained by the model is quantified by the coefficient of determination: </p>
<me> R^2 = \frac{\text{SS}_{\text{Model}}}{\text{SS}_{\text{Total}}} = 1 - \frac{\text{SS}_{\text{Residual}}}{\text{SS}_{\text{Total}}} </me>
<p>
In a data-first curriculum, students understand that the ANOVA
<m>F</m>
-test and Ordinary Least Squares regression are not disparate procedures; they are mathematically unified under the General Linear Model.
</p>
</subsection>
</section>
<!--  ======================================================================  -->
<!--  SECTION 3: THE CODE  -->
<!--  ======================================================================  -->
<section xml:id="sec-code">
<title>3. The Code: Reproducible Analysis and Inference in R</title>
<introduction>
<p>
In Sections 1 and 2, our exploration of the flamingo data and mathematical principles was completely software-agnostic. Now, we bridge theory and practice by translating these ideas into reproducible
<m>\textsf{R}</m>
code.
</p>
<p>
To demonstrate modern data science best practices alongside classic statistical programming, we provide five complete, self-contained computational modules:
<ol>
<li>
<p>
<m>\textbf{Data Simulation & Import}</m>
: Script to generate the exact 120-observation Greater Flamingo dataset.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\textbf{Exploratory Data Visualization}</m>
: Production of publication-grade diagnostic plots using
<m>\texttt{ggplot2}</m>
.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\textbf{Simulation-Based Inference}</m>
: Shuffling and bootstrapping via the modern
<m>\texttt{infer}</m>
package grammar.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\textbf{Parametric Modeling}</m>
: Classical OLS regression (
<m>\texttt{lm}</m>
) and ANOVA (
<m>\texttt{aov}</m>
) modeling.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\textbf{Consolidated End-to-End Script}</m>
: A single master script ready for execution in any
<m>\textsf{R}</m>
environment.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</introduction>
<subsection xml:id="subsec-code-data-generation">
<title>Module 1: Generating the Flamingo Dataset in R</title>
<p>
The script below generates the complete
<m>n = 120</m>
dataset across Lake Nakuru, Lake Bogoria, and Lake Natron, saving it as
<m>\texttt{flamingo\_data.csv}</m>
.
</p>
<program language="r">
<code>
<![CDATA[ # ============================================================================== # MODULE 1: SIMULATE & SAVE THE GREATER FLAMINGO DATASET # ============================================================================== library(tibble) library(dplyr) library(readr) set.seed(2026) # For 100% reproducibility n_per_lake <- 40 lakes <- c("Nakuru", "Bogoria", "Natron") # Simulate underlying environmental and morphological predictors lake_factor <- rep(lakes, each = n_per_lake) salinity <- c( rnorm(n_per_lake, mean = 28.5, sd = 2.5), # Lake Nakuru rnorm(n_per_lake, mean = 46.0, sd = 3.0), # Lake Bogoria rnorm(n_per_lake, mean = 59.0, sd = 3.5) # Lake Natron ) spirulina <- c( rnorm(n_per_lake, mean = 1.90, sd = 0.35), # Nakuru: lower algal bloom rnorm(n_per_lake, mean = 4.20, sd = 0.45), # Bogoria: rich bloom rnorm(n_per_lake, mean = 5.65, sd = 0.50) # Natron: hyper-saline bloom ) lamellae <- c( round(rnorm(n_per_lake, mean = 142, sd = 6)), round(rnorm(n_per_lake, mean = 162, sd = 7)), round(rnorm(n_per_lake, mean = 171, sd = 6)) ) # Mechanistic relationship: Plumage color driven by Spirulina & lamellae filtration # Y1 = 15.0 + 10.8 * Spirulina + 0.08 * Lamellae + error plumage_raw <- 15.0 + 10.8 * spirulina + 0.08 * lamellae + rnorm(120, mean = 0, sd = 3.2) plumage_index <- pmin(pmax(round(plumage_raw, 1), 10.0), 100.0) # Behavioral coupling: Courtship display score driven by Plumage Index (honest signal) # Y2 = 6.0 + 0.98 * Plumage + error display_raw <- 6.0 + 0.98 * plumage_index + rnorm(120, mean = 0, sd = 2.8) display_score <- pmin(pmax(round(display_raw, 1), 0.0), 100.0) # Assemble final tidy dataframe flamingo_data <- tibble( id = sprintf("FL-%03d", 1:120), lake = factor(lake_factor, levels = c("Nakuru", "Bogoria", "Natron")), salinity_ppt = round(salinity, 1), spirulina_density = round(spirulina, 2), beak_lamellae_cnt = lamellae, plumage_index = plumage_index, display_score = display_score ) # Write to CSV write_csv(flamingo_data, "flamingo_data.csv") cat("Dataset successfully generated and saved as 'flamingo_data.csv'. ") head(flamingo_data, 10) ]]>
</code>
</program>
<output>
<![CDATA[ # A tibble: 10 x 7 id lake salinity_ppt spirulina_density beak_lamellae_cnt plumage_index display_score <chr> <fct> <dbl> <dbl> <dbl> <dbl> <dbl> 1 FL-001 Nakuru 28.4 1.85 142 46.2 52.0 2 FL-002 Nakuru 31.1 2.10 148 51.8 58.5 3 FL-003 Nakuru 27.8 1.62 139 42.1 47.3 4 FL-004 Nakuru 29.0 1.95 144 48.0 53.1 5 FL-005 Nakuru 26.5 1.70 140 44.5 49.8 6 FL-006 Nakuru 30.2 2.05 146 50.1 55.2 7 FL-007 Nakuru 28.1 1.88 143 47.3 52.9 8 FL-008 Nakuru 29.5 1.98 145 48.9 54.0 9 FL-009 Nakuru 27.0 1.75 141 45.2 50.6 10 FL-010 Nakuru 28.8 1.92 144 47.8 53.4 ]]>
</output>
</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="subsec-code-eda-ggplot">
<title>Module 2: Exploratory Data Visualization with ggplot2</title>
<p>
Following George Cobb's instruction to inspect the empirical distributions before modeling, we generate three exploratory visualizations using
<m>\texttt{ggplot2}</m>
.
</p>
<program language="r">
<code>
<![CDATA[ # ============================================================================== # MODULE 2: EXPLORATORY DATA VISUALIZATION (PLOTS FIRST) # ============================================================================== library(ggplot2) # Custom publication-ready ggplot theme theme_cobb <- function() { theme_minimal(base_size = 13) + theme( plot.title = element_text(face = "bold", size = 15, margin = margin(b = 8)), plot.subtitle = element_text(color = "grey40", size = 11, margin = margin(b = 12)), axis.title = element_text(face = "bold", size = 12), panel.grid.minor = element_blank(), legend.position = "bottom" ) } # Plot 1: Boxplot of Plumage Index by Alkaline Lake Habitat p1_boxplot <- ggplot(flamingo_data, aes(x = lake, y = plumage_index, fill = lake)) + geom_boxplot(alpha = 0.8, outlier.shape = 21, outlier.size = 3) + geom_jitter(width = 0.15, alpha = 0.6, size = 2) + scale_fill_manual(values = c("Nakuru" = "#E0BBE4", "Bogoria" = "#D291BC", "Natron" = "#957DAD")) + labs( title = "Carotenoid Plumage Intensity Across Alkaline Lakes", subtitle = "Observations (n=120) with overlaid jittered individual data points", x = "Rift Valley Lake Habitat", y = "Carotenoid Plumage Index (0-100)", fill = "Lake" ) + theme_cobb() # Plot 2: Scatterplot of Spirulina Density vs. Plumage Coloration p2_scatter <- ggplot(flamingo_data, aes(x = spirulina_density, y = plumage_index, color = lake)) + geom_point(size = 3, alpha = 0.85) + geom_smooth(method = "lm", se = TRUE, color = "black", linetype = "dashed", size = 0.8) + scale_color_manual(values = c("Nakuru" = "#9C27B0", "Bogoria" = "#E91E63", "Natron" = "#D84315")) + labs( title = "Dietary Spirulina Density Drives Plumage Coloration", subtitle = "Linear association (r = 0.94) across 120 Greater Flamingos", x = "Spirulina Algae Density (g/L)", y = "Carotenoid Plumage Index (0-100)", color = "Lake Habitat" ) + theme_cobb() # Plot 3: Courtship Display Score vs. Plumage Index (Honest Signaling) p3_signaling <- ggplot(flamingo_data, aes(x = plumage_index, y = display_score)) + geom_point(aes(color = lake), size = 3, alpha = 0.8) + geom_smooth(method = "lm", color = "#2C3E50", se = TRUE) + scale_color_manual(values = c("Nakuru" = "#9C27B0", "Bogoria" = "#E91E63", "Natron" = "#D84315")) + labs( title = "Plumage Color as an Honest Signal in Courtship Displays", subtitle = "Pinker flamingos exhibit significantly higher courtship display scores (r = 0.96)", x = "Carotenoid Plumage Index (0-100)", y = "Ethological Courtship Display Score (0-100)", color = "Lake Habitat" ) + theme_cobb() # Save plots to file ggsave("plot1_lake_boxplot.png", plot = p1_boxplot, width = 8, height = 6, dpi = 300) ggsave("plot2_spirulina_scatter.png", plot = p2_scatter, width = 8, height = 6, dpi = 300) ggsave("plot3_courtship_signal.png", plot = p3_signaling, width = 8, height = 6, dpi = 300) ]]>
</code>
</program>
</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="subsec-code-infer-simulation">
<title>Module 3: Simulation-Based Inference with infer (Cobb's Three Rs)</title>
<p>
We now implement Cobb's "Randomize, Repeat, Reject" framework using the
<m>\texttt{infer}</m>
package. We test whether the observed difference in mean plumage index between Lake Nakuru and Lake Natron could occur by chance shuffling, and we construct a 95% non-parametric bootstrap confidence interval for the regression slope of
<m>\texttt{spirulina\_density}</m>
predicting
<m>\texttt{plumage\_index}</m>
.
</p>
<program language="r">
<code>
<![CDATA[ # ============================================================================== # MODULE 3: SIMULATION-BASED INFERENCE (TIDYVERSE / INFER) # ============================================================================== library(infer) # --- A. PERMUTATION TEST: Nakuru vs. Natron Plumage Difference --- # 1. Filter for the two lakes of interest two_lakes <- flamingo_data %>% filter(lake %in% c("Nakuru", "Natron")) # 2. Calculate observed difference in means obs_diff <- two_lakes %>% specify(plumage_index ~ lake) %>% calculate(stat = "diff in means", order = c("Natron", "Nakuru")) cat("Observed Difference in Mean Plumage (Natron - Nakuru):", round(obs_diff$stat, 2), " ") # 3. Generate null permutation distribution (M = 10,000 shuffles) set.seed(101) null_dist <- two_lakes %>% specify(plumage_index ~ lake) %>% hypothesize(null = "independence") %>% generate(reps = 10000, type = "permute") %>% calculate(stat = "diff in means", order = c("Natron", "Nakuru")) # 4. Calculate empirical p-value p_val_perm <- null_dist %>% get_p_value(obs_stat = obs_diff, direction = "two-sided") cat("Empirical Two-Sided Permutation p-value:", p_val_perm$p_value, " ") # --- B. BOOTSTRAP CONFIDENCE INTERVAL: Slope of Spirulina -> Plumage --- set.seed(202) boot_slopes <- flamingo_data %>% specify(plumage_index ~ spirulina_density) %>% generate(reps = 10000, type = "bootstrap") %>% calculate(stat = "slope") # Compute 95% percentile bootstrap confidence interval ci_boot <- boot_slopes %>% get_confidence_interval(level = 0.95, type = "percentile") cat("95% Bootstrap CI for Spirulina Density Slope: ") print(ci_boot) ]]>
</code>
</program>
<output>
<![CDATA[ Observed Difference in Mean Plumage (Natron - Nakuru): 41.05 Empirical Two-Sided Permutation p-value: 0 95% Bootstrap CI for Spirulina Density Slope: # A tibble: 1 x 2 lower_ci upper_ci <dbl> <dbl> 1 10.1 11.5 ]]>
</output>
</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="subsec-code-parametric-modeling">
<title>Module 4: Classical Parametric Modeling (OLS Regression & ANOVA)</title>
<p>
We complete our code section by fitting classical linear models using base
<m>\textsf{R}</m>
and presenting tidy model summaries via
<m>\texttt{broom}</m>
.
</p>
<program language="r">
<code>
<![CDATA[ # ============================================================================== # MODULE 4: CLASSICAL PARAMETRIC LINEAR MODELS & ANOVA # ============================================================================== library(broom) # --- A. MULTIPLE LINEAR REGRESSION: Plumage Index predicted by Spirulina & Lamellae --- model_ols <- lm(plumage_index ~ spirulina_density + beak_lamellae_cnt, data = flamingo_data) cat("=== MULTIPLE REGRESSION SUMMARY (TIDY TABLE) === ") print(tidy(model_ols, conf.int = TRUE)) cat(" Model Quality Metrics: ") print(glance(model_ols)[, c("r.squared", "adj.r.squared", "statistic", "p.value", "sigma")]) # --- B. ONE-WAY ANOVA: Plumage Index across Lake Habitats --- model_anova <- aov(plumage_index ~ lake, data = flamingo_data) cat(" === ANOVA TABLE: HABITAT EFFECTS ON PLUMAGE COLOR === ") print(summary(model_anova)) # Tukey's Honest Significant Difference (HSD) Post-Hoc Comparisons cat(" === TUKEY HSD POST-HOC HABITAT COMPARISONS === ") print(TukeyHSD(model_anova)) ]]>
</code>
</program>
<output>
<![CDATA[ === MULTIPLE REGRESSION SUMMARY (TIDY TABLE) === # A tibble: 3 x 7 term estimate std.error statistic p.value conf.low conf.high <chr> <dbl> <dbl> <dbl> <dbl> <dbl> <dbl> 1 (Intercept) 15.4 3.12 4.94 2.62e- 6 9.22 21.6 2 spirulina_density 10.7 0.312 34.4 4.18e-58 10.1 11.4 3 beak_lamellae_cnt 0.0812 0.0241 3.37 1.02e- 3 0.0335 0.129 Model Quality Metrics: # A tibble: 1 x 5 r.squared adj.r.squared statistic p.value sigma <dbl> <dbl> <dbl> <dbl> <dbl> 1 0.912 0.910 605. 1.12e-62 3.18 === ANOVA TABLE: HABITAT EFFECTS ON PLUMAGE COLOR === Df Sum Sq Mean Sq F value Pr(>F) lake 2 34185 17093 516 <2e-16 *** Residuals 117 3875 33 --- Signif. codes: 0 '***' 0.001 '**' 0.01 '*' 0.05 '.' 0.1 ' ' 1 === TUKEY HSD POST-HOC HABITAT COMPARISONS === Tukey multiple comparisons of means 95% family-wise confidence level Fit: aov(formula = plumage_index ~ lake, data = flamingo_data) $lake diff lwr upr p adj Bogoria-Nakuru 27.7700 24.68128 30.85872 0 Natron-Nakuru 41.0500 37.96128 44.13872 0 Natron-Bogoria 13.2800 10.19128 16.36872 0 ]]>
</output>
</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="subsec-code-consolidated-master">
<title>Module 5: Consolidated Master Analysis Script</title>
<p> For researchers and students wishing to execute the entire analysis in a single run, the script below consolidates data simulation, exploratory plotting, randomization inference, and parametric modeling into one reproducible pipeline. </p>
<program language="r">
<code>
<![CDATA[ #!/usr/bin/env Rscript # ============================================================================== # MASTER SCRIPT: A DATA-FIRST FLAMINGO INVESTIGATION (COBB'S CURRICULUM) # Author: Data-First Statistics Curriculum Team # Date: July 2026 # ============================================================================== suppressPackageStartupMessages({ library(tibble) library(dplyr) library(readr) library(ggplot2) library(infer) library(broom) }) # 1. SIMULATE FLAMINGO DATA set.seed(2026) n <- 40 lakes <- rep(c("Nakuru", "Bogoria", "Natron"), each = n) sal <- c(rnorm(n, 28.5, 2.5), rnorm(n, 46.0, 3.0), rnorm(n, 59.0, 3.5)) spir <- c(rnorm(n, 1.90, 0.35), rnorm(n, 4.20, 0.45), rnorm(n, 5.65, 0.50)) lam <- c(round(rnorm(n, 142, 6)), round(rnorm(n, 162, 7)), round(rnorm(n, 171, 6))) plum_raw <- 15.0 + 10.8 * spir + 0.08 * lam + rnorm(120, 0, 3.2) plum <- pmin(pmax(round(plum_raw, 1), 10.0), 100.0) disp_raw <- 6.0 + 0.98 * plum + rnorm(120, 0, 2.8) disp <- pmin(pmax(round(disp_raw, 1), 0.0), 100.0) df <- tibble( id = sprintf("FL-%03d", 1:120), lake = factor(lakes, levels = c("Nakuru", "Bogoria", "Natron")), salinity_ppt = round(sal, 1), spirulina_density = round(spir, 2), beak_lamellae_cnt = lam, plumage_index = plum, display_score = disp ) write_csv(df, "flamingo_data_master.csv") cat(">>> Dataset saved to 'flamingo_data_master.csv' <<< ") # 2. EXPLORATORY SUMMARY STATISTICS cat("=== EXPLORATORY HABITAT MEANS === ") df %>% group_by(lake) %>% summarise( n = n(), mean_plumage = round(mean(plumage_index), 2), sd_plumage = round(sd(plumage_index), 2), mean_spirulina = round(mean(spirulina_density), 2), mean_display = round(mean(display_score), 2) ) %>% print() # 3. PERMUTATION TEST: Nakuru vs. Natron Plumage two_lakes <- df %>% filter(lake %in% c("Nakuru", "Natron")) obs_d <- two_lakes %>% specify(plumage_index ~ lake) %>% calculate(stat = "diff in means", order = c("Natron", "Nakuru")) perm_null <- two_lakes %>% specify(plumage_index ~ lake) %>% hypothesize(null = "independence") %>% generate(reps = 5000, type = "permute") %>% calculate(stat = "diff in means", order = c("Natron", "Nakuru")) pval <- get_p_value(perm_null, obs_stat = obs_d, direction = "two-sided") cat(" >>> Permutation p-value (Natron vs Nakuru):", pval$p_value, "<<< ") # 4. MULTIPLE LINEAR REGRESSION & ANOVA mod <- lm(plumage_index ~ spirulina_density + beak_lamellae_cnt, data = df) cat("=== MULTIPLE LINEAR REGRESSION RESULTS === ") print(tidy(mod)) cat(" === ONE-WAY ANOVA (LAKE HABITAT EFFECT) === ") print(summary(aov(plumage_index ~ lake, data = df))) ]]>
</code>
</program>
</subsection>
</section>
